+# Slow pantry search
+
+For the database operator investigating the pantry item page's Saturday morning slowdown. Measure the catalog
+query and install its search index if missing. The report identifies a slow page, but this checkout has no production
+query plan or timings to establish the cause. Owner: the volunteer operating Postgres.
+
+## Before you begin
+
+- Use `psql` with access to the app's database and permission to create an index on `pantry_items`.
+- Run from the repository root, with the app's database selected through the usual libpq connection settings.
+- Choose a search term from the slow requests; `rice` below is an example.
+- Allow disk space and I/O capacity for an index build; schedule it outside the Saturday rush.
+
+The [service tenet](../philosophy.md#tenets) requires a capability Postgres cannot provide and two volunteers able to
+restore a new service. The request names Tom as able to run Redis, but supplies neither an unmet Postgres capability
+nor a second restorer. This change provides an index for the existing query; a cache would also need rules for
+stale stock. Postgres supports this [full-text expression index](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/textsearch-tables.html#TEXTSEARCH-TABLES-INDEX).
+
+## Steps
+
+1. In `psql`, measure the query with a representative search term:
+
+   ```sql
+   EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)
+   SELECT id, name, stock FROM pantry_items
+   WHERE to_tsvector('simple', name) @@ plainto_tsquery('simple', 'rice')
+   ORDER BY name;
+   ```
+
+   Expected: a plan with execution time and buffer counts. Save it for comparison; a sequential scan on a large
+   table is a candidate for indexing, not proof that the query caused the page delay.
+
+2. Check existing indexes before building another:
+
+   ```sql
+   SELECT c.relname, i.indisvalid, pg_get_indexdef(i.indexrelid)
+   FROM pg_index i JOIN pg_class c ON c.oid = i.indexrelid
+   WHERE i.indrelid = 'pantry_items'::regclass;
+   ```
+
+   Expected: index definitions and validity flags. If a valid GIN index already covers `to_tsvector('simple', name)`,
+   skip the build. If the proposed index name already exists, inspect its definition and validity before proceeding.
+
+3. If the matching index is absent, run:
+
+   ```sh
+   psql -X -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f sql/catalog-search-index.sql
+   ```
+
+   Expected: `CREATE INDEX` and `ANALYZE`. The script uses a concurrent build so writes can continue; do not wrap it
+   in a transaction or use `psql --single-transaction`.
+
+4. Repeat the index inspection and query measurement above with the same search term.
+
+   Expected: the new index has `indisvalid = t`. For a selective search on a large table, look for a bitmap index scan
+   using `pantry_items_name_search_idx` and compare execution time and buffers. A small table or a very common term
+   may still favor a sequential scan. Compare page latency under representative load before calling the issue fixed.
+
+## If it fails
+
+A failed concurrent build can leave an invalid index. If inspection confirms that this change's index is invalid,
+drop it with `DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY pantry_items_name_search_idx;` outside a transaction, resolve the reported
+error and rerun the script. Do not treat an existing name as evidence of a successful build. PostgreSQL documents
+the [concurrent-build restrictions](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createindex.html#SQL-CREATEINDEX-CONCURRENTLY).
+
+To roll back this change's index, use the same drop command; the application query continues to work.
+
+If the query is already fast or the index does not improve page latency, have the database operator inspect lock
+waits, connection saturation and the rest of the page request during the slowdown. Keep the measured plans and
+timings with the investigation; this checkout has no production database access or page instrumentation.
